1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:05,000
It's fresh orange juice thousands of miles from the Grove.

2
00:00:05,001 --> 00:00:10,000
It ensures that the first hand to touch that morning egg is yours.

3
00:00:10,001 --> 00:00:19,000
It slices bacon, cooked toast, and inserts that little maple nugget inside a McGriddle sandwich.

4
00:00:19,001 --> 00:00:23,000
And it's a mega business worth tens of billions of dollars.

5
00:00:23,001 --> 00:00:27,000
Now, Breakfast Tech on Modern Marvels.

6
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:44,000
At around 01.30, that's when we start the preparation for breakfast.

7
00:00:49,100 --> 00:00:56,460
We pull out our bacon, we pull out our fresh eggs, all our products.

8
00:00:56,461 --> 00:01:00,340
in one of six galleys aboard the USS John C. Stennis.

9
00:01:00,341 --> 00:01:03,020
38 food service personnel are preparing breakfast

10
00:01:03,021 --> 00:01:04,700
for a small navy.

11
00:01:04,701 --> 00:01:08,260
We serve about almost 5,000 personnel.

12
00:01:08,261 --> 00:01:13,660
And the challenge is we have to be managing our time wisely

13
00:01:13,661 --> 00:01:14,820
as far as feeding personnel.

14
00:01:18,060 --> 00:01:19,540
On a typical morning, the sailors

15
00:01:19,541 --> 00:01:24,100
will consume 10,000 eggs, 3,100 strips of bacon,

16
00:01:24,101 --> 00:01:31,100
250 gallons of juice, 1,800 pancakes, and 215 pounds of potatoes.

17
00:01:31,101 --> 00:01:33,370
Technology has helped us out.

18
00:01:33,371 --> 00:01:40,100
We have frozen foods on board and have the same quality and nutrition for the sailors.

19
00:01:40,101 --> 00:01:47,100
There's a lot of pre-prepared food that it minimizes the preparation time.

20
00:01:47,101 --> 00:01:53,100
These sailors enjoy a tasty and nutritious breakfast despite being 100 miles out at sea.

21
00:01:53,101 --> 00:01:59,940
The credit lies with past innovators who found ways to blend food science and technology

22
00:01:59,941 --> 00:02:02,940
with mother nature.

23
00:02:02,941 --> 00:02:09,360
And now, thanks to that blend, more people than ever have a variety of breakfast foods

24
00:02:09,361 --> 00:02:13,240
to choose from.

25
00:02:13,241 --> 00:02:17,580
In America, breakfasts reflect a melting pot of regional delights.

26
00:02:17,581 --> 00:02:20,960
One breakfast stands above the rest, bacon and eggs.

27
00:02:23,140 --> 00:02:28,700
In America's early years, bacon and eggs was really the breakfast of farmers,

28
00:02:28,980 --> 00:02:31,760
of people who lived near those ingredients.

29
00:02:31,761 --> 00:02:33,920
That's where they could get fresh food.

30
00:02:34,480 --> 00:02:39,000
And those lucky, wealthy people who could have eggs and bacon

31
00:02:39,001 --> 00:02:41,620
freshly brought into their estates and homes.

32
00:02:42,980 --> 00:02:47,400
In the 1930s, refrigeration made bacon and eggs available to more people.

33
00:02:47,580 --> 00:02:51,580
But Americans had yet to fully embrace it as their breakfast of choice.

34
00:02:51,581 --> 00:02:57,580
Enter Edward Bernays, the man who turned bacon and eggs into an American tradition.

35
00:02:57,581 --> 00:03:01,580
He's called the founder of modern PR, public relations.

36
00:03:01,581 --> 00:03:03,580
He worked for the bacon industry.

37
00:03:03,581 --> 00:03:08,580
He sent out this interesting questionnaire to over 5,000 doctors across America.

38
00:03:08,581 --> 00:03:14,580
Bernays' crafty questionnaire asked the doctors if a hearty breakfast was good for Americans.

39
00:03:14,581 --> 00:03:19,580
The doctors overwhelmingly backed Bernays' ambiguous inquiry.

40
00:03:19,581 --> 00:03:23,580
Bernays then sold the public on his version of a hearty breakfast.

41
00:03:23,581 --> 00:03:25,580
Bacon and eggs.

42
00:03:25,581 --> 00:03:26,580
America bought it.

43
00:03:26,581 --> 00:03:29,580
Modern technology made it a sensation.

44
00:03:29,581 --> 00:03:37,580
In 2005, nearly 90 million hogs were slaughtered in the U.S. for human consumption.

45
00:03:37,581 --> 00:03:40,580
Many of them ended up at the Tyson Pork Factory,

46
00:03:40,581 --> 00:03:44,580
a leading producer that has been making bacon since the 1970s.

47
00:03:44,581 --> 00:03:48,580
Pork bellies are received in the facility on refrigerated semi-trailers.

48
00:03:48,581 --> 00:03:52,580
This facility will run about 600,000 pounds in a day's time.

49
00:03:52,581 --> 00:04:00,580
The time from harvest that the pork bellies are harvested from the animal until it comes in to this facility is about one day.

50
00:04:04,580 --> 00:04:10,580
In the U.S., bacon slices are culled from the two bellies of a hog, located on each side of the animal.

51
00:04:11,520 --> 00:04:17,420
The English carve their bacon from the bellies and the back of the animal, where the fat content is considerably less.

52
00:04:18,700 --> 00:04:24,600
Canadian bacon, the leanest of all, is also taken from the back, but from the tender center loin portion.

53
00:04:25,880 --> 00:04:31,320
However it's sliced, once it reaches the factory, the pig's final journey to the breakfast table is well underway.

54
00:04:31,960 --> 00:04:38,520
In this part of the process, the bellies are being staged to remove the skin and begin the injection process.

55
00:04:38,521 --> 00:04:44,340
It goes through a heavy roller which only flattens the belly out so that we can allow

56
00:04:44,341 --> 00:04:47,100
for proper removal of the skin.

57
00:04:47,101 --> 00:04:52,280
This piece of equipment has a series of rollers and a knife that will remove about 3 eighths

58
00:04:52,281 --> 00:04:57,860
of an inch of the belly that also removes the skin from the belly.

59
00:04:57,861 --> 00:05:05,700
Once the skin is removed, it's transferred on this conveyor to another packing station.

60
00:05:05,701 --> 00:05:13,540
These skins are used for gelatin use or popping for the production of pork rinds.

61
00:05:13,541 --> 00:05:17,880
Before modern processing, preserving bacon meant soaking the pork in salt or brine for

62
00:05:17,881 --> 00:05:20,540
several days.

63
00:05:20,541 --> 00:05:25,160
This prevented the meat from spoiling by pulling out the moisture and creating a barren environment

64
00:05:25,161 --> 00:05:29,660
for bacteria growth.

65
00:05:29,661 --> 00:05:34,120
In modern bacon curing, a blend of chemicals is injected into the bellies, creating the

66
00:05:34,121 --> 00:05:37,880
same reaction as salt and brine in mere hours.

67
00:05:37,881 --> 00:05:42,520
This is where the curing ingredients are injected into the pork belly.

68
00:05:42,521 --> 00:05:49,680
Inside this injector there are 168 needles that are injecting a solution of water, salt,

69
00:05:49,681 --> 00:05:56,360
sugar, sodium phosphate, sodium erothorate, and sodium nitrite into the belly.

70
00:05:56,361 --> 00:06:02,440
The water acts as a medium to transfer the ingredients into the belly.

71
00:06:02,441 --> 00:06:04,800
The salt and sugar are flavoring agents.

72
00:06:05,320 --> 00:06:09,480
Sodium phosphate is a processing aid that allows moisture retention.

73
00:06:10,740 --> 00:06:13,720
Sodium erythrobate and sodium nitrite are the curing agents.

74
00:06:15,680 --> 00:06:19,800
Before these pig bellies go to market, they enter the thermal processing unit,

75
00:06:20,200 --> 00:06:22,640
where they're heated at 135 degrees Fahrenheit,

76
00:06:23,180 --> 00:06:26,840
and the aroma of smoldering wood chips is introduced to add a smoky flavor.

77
00:06:26,841 --> 00:06:35,600
once they've cooled for 36 to 72 hours it's down the stretch they go and into a stainless steel

78
00:06:35,601 --> 00:06:43,280
labyrinth of modern machinery first the bellies are pressed into rectangular blocks and prepared

79
00:06:43,281 --> 00:06:53,960
for slicing as the belly works its way through the slicer it crosses the slicing blade which

80
00:06:53,961 --> 00:06:57,460
is traveling at 1,500 revolutions per minute.

81
00:06:57,461 --> 00:07:01,700
As this bacon is being sliced into one pound units,

82
00:07:01,701 --> 00:07:05,860
it's also calculating the net weight of that package of bacon

83
00:07:05,861 --> 00:07:09,280
with the number of predetermined slices in each package.

84
00:07:09,281 --> 00:07:13,080
As it moves across this area of the slicer,

85
00:07:13,081 --> 00:07:17,800
the bacon transfers on to the primary label of the product,

86
00:07:17,801 --> 00:07:20,960
which also allows the bacon draft to move down

87
00:07:20,961 --> 00:07:23,240
the processing line.

88
00:07:23,241 --> 00:07:26,820
At the end of the line, the bacon slices are vacuum-packed and ready for market.

89
00:07:27,780 --> 00:07:29,060
What's in one thick slice?

90
00:07:30,320 --> 00:07:34,440
175 calories, 25% of the FDA's daily allowance for fat,

91
00:07:34,580 --> 00:07:36,780
and 9% of the recommended amount of protein.

92
00:07:39,100 --> 00:07:43,880
The process of putting the other half of the great American breakfast on the plate is equally industrial.

93
00:07:46,760 --> 00:07:48,700
Chickens weren't indigenous to America.

94
00:07:48,701 --> 00:07:52,900
The ones the Pilgrims bought, those chickens, those good layers from their hometowns,

95
00:07:52,901 --> 00:07:57,580
were really the beginning of our egg industry.

96
00:07:57,581 --> 00:07:59,940
America's egg industry produces and distributes

97
00:07:59,941 --> 00:08:03,320
over 75 billion eggs a year.

98
00:08:03,321 --> 00:08:05,840
Such volume takes a little dose of science

99
00:08:05,841 --> 00:08:10,600
and a heavy helping of reproductive manipulation.

100
00:08:10,601 --> 00:08:13,420
Eggland's Best in Pennsylvania is on the cutting edge

101
00:08:13,421 --> 00:08:15,240
of today's egg industry.

102
00:08:15,241 --> 00:08:18,180
Science has surely brought egg production a long way

103
00:08:18,181 --> 00:08:22,860
from what it was 100 or 200 years ago when hens

104
00:08:22,861 --> 00:08:26,820
were just foraging for whatever kind of food items they could get.

105
00:08:28,380 --> 00:08:31,960
The first rule of thumb in commercial chicken farming is uncomplicated.

106
00:08:32,600 --> 00:08:34,840
What goes in a hen comes out in its egg.

107
00:08:36,180 --> 00:08:40,520
To that end, today's hens are fed a well-rounded diet of corn, soybean meal,

108
00:08:40,920 --> 00:08:42,560
and a variety of minerals and vitamins.

109
00:08:44,340 --> 00:08:47,040
But some hens are naturally more productive than others,

110
00:08:47,400 --> 00:08:49,520
like these at Eglin's best test farm.

111
00:08:49,521 --> 00:08:54,000
This breed of chicken is called white leghorn.

112
00:08:54,280 --> 00:08:57,860
It's been selected for many years for a high rate of egg production.

113
00:08:58,720 --> 00:09:02,100
And they will lay about 270 eggs per year.

114
00:09:02,640 --> 00:09:06,080
And they will do this very efficiently for about two years.

115
00:09:08,240 --> 00:09:12,800
Getting hens to lay almost 300 eggs per year does, however, require some assistance.

116
00:09:13,920 --> 00:09:14,620
Light.

117
00:09:14,621 --> 00:09:19,520
The mechanism of light stimulation inside a hen is a photoreceptor.

118
00:09:20,440 --> 00:09:23,520
When the day length and the intensity of light increase,

119
00:09:24,360 --> 00:09:28,760
the photoreceptor perceives this increase in day length and intensity

120
00:09:28,761 --> 00:09:32,840
and brings about an increase in the hormones that are responsible

121
00:09:32,841 --> 00:09:37,160
for the maturation of the hen that leads to egg production.

122
00:09:38,560 --> 00:09:42,860
Caged chickens will spend their entire lives soaking up light inside a hen house.

123
00:09:44,520 --> 00:09:47,940
Cage-free hens can roam around open, lighted barns,

124
00:09:48,200 --> 00:09:53,640
while free-range chickens are allowed to wander outside the barn for short periods.

125
00:09:55,620 --> 00:10:00,960
Regardless of the accommodations, after about two years, the fertility of the hens begins to wane,

126
00:10:01,560 --> 00:10:03,500
and younger hens are brought in to replace them.

127
00:10:05,120 --> 00:10:13,720
These hens will be used for pet food or other food items like meat pies or chicken soup.

128
00:10:13,721 --> 00:10:17,020
And then a new group of hens will have to be used for egg production.

129
00:10:18,460 --> 00:10:24,020
But long before the laying hens buy the farm, their eggs enter the process of commercial production.

130
00:10:26,260 --> 00:10:33,060
At an Eglin's best packaging facility, eggs drop from cages onto a conveyor that transports them through a tunnel system.

131
00:10:35,420 --> 00:10:40,280
The fragile cargo glides smoothly over oiled rollers and into the processing plant.

132
00:10:40,281 --> 00:10:47,280
Here we're at the beginning stage where the eggs are actually gathered.

133
00:10:47,281 --> 00:10:50,280
This whole system is controlled by sensors.

134
00:10:50,281 --> 00:10:55,280
If there's a buildup of eggs, this belt will stop while the machine still runs,

135
00:10:55,281 --> 00:11:01,280
which allows us to clear this area to allow more eggs to flow through the conveyor system.

136
00:11:01,281 --> 00:11:06,280
First, the conveyor carries the eggs through a series of tanks where any soiling matter is removed.

137
00:11:06,281 --> 00:11:12,800
And in this tank, we also have a sanitizer, which is a chlorine base with water.

138
00:11:13,320 --> 00:11:19,520
In this pipe here, the water is actually 120 degrees for our final rinse to make sure the egg is sanitary.

139
00:11:20,820 --> 00:11:24,040
Once the eggs are blown dry, they enter a candling booth.

140
00:11:24,940 --> 00:11:30,080
Here, they move over a set of lights that allow the candler to see past the shell and into the egg.

141
00:11:30,980 --> 00:11:34,000
The candler looks for blood spots and other imperfections.

142
00:11:34,001 --> 00:11:38,000
Any eggs that fail the test are discarded.

143
00:11:38,001 --> 00:11:44,000
Cracked eggs are plucked from the line and later turned into liquid and powder products.

144
00:11:44,001 --> 00:11:49,000
Eggs that make the grade continue through the system where they're weighed by a computer

145
00:11:49,001 --> 00:11:52,000
and directed into the appropriate basket for packaging.

146
00:11:52,001 --> 00:11:57,000
The eggs are placed into the cartons face down as this for two reasons.

147
00:11:57,001 --> 00:12:02,000
One, there's an air sack at the top of the egg, so the egg by gravity falls face down.

148
00:12:02,001 --> 00:12:06,820
This allows us for a larger surface to be stamped with when they're stamped,

149
00:12:06,940 --> 00:12:09,360
and also the bottom of the egg's a little stronger,

150
00:12:09,720 --> 00:12:12,480
so when they're placed into the carton, it allows for less breakage.

151
00:12:15,040 --> 00:12:19,940
Locked and loaded, the cartons are carefully packed on pallets and stored at 45 degrees.

152
00:12:23,200 --> 00:12:25,140
But before their trip to the supermarket,

153
00:12:25,880 --> 00:12:29,080
random samples are taken by on-site government food inspectors,

154
00:12:29,081 --> 00:12:30,740
who check the eggs for safety.

155
00:12:32,000 --> 00:12:36,000
Nutritionally, the standard American egg is a bit of a contradiction.

156
00:12:36,001 --> 00:12:42,000
80 calories and 10% of the recommended daily amount of protein make it lean and healthy.

157
00:12:42,001 --> 00:12:47,000
But with 66% of the daily limit on cholesterol, eggs have some health professionals scrambling.

158
00:12:47,001 --> 00:12:54,000
The egg industry has responded with products like egg beaters that use only the cholesterol-free egg whites.

159
00:12:54,001 --> 00:13:01,000
Other companies like Eggland's Best have gone even further with the development of a scientifically enhanced designer egg.

160
00:13:01,001 --> 00:13:07,560
The nutritional composition of an egg can be varied considerably according to the diet

161
00:13:07,561 --> 00:13:08,560
of the hen.

162
00:13:08,561 --> 00:13:14,000
It makes it possible to increase the omega-3 fatty acids, to increase most vitamins, a

163
00:13:14,001 --> 00:13:20,980
lot of minerals and different antioxidants as well to make the egg more healthy for us.

164
00:13:20,981 --> 00:13:25,900
By changing the hen's diet with the addition of the good fat known as omega-3, overall

165
00:13:25,901 --> 00:13:34,080
cholesterol from a generic egg can be lowered by about 20%.

166
00:13:34,081 --> 00:13:38,640
While adapting to the health conscious consumer, America's favorite hearty breakfast seems

167
00:13:38,641 --> 00:13:41,560
to have a golden future.

168
00:13:41,561 --> 00:13:45,940
And when it comes to health, there's nothing better than starting America's favorite breakfast

169
00:13:45,941 --> 00:13:51,560
with a glass of orange juice.

170
00:13:51,561 --> 00:13:55,320
In commercial egg production, the color of an eggshell reflects the color of the hen's

171
00:13:55,321 --> 00:13:56,320
feathers.

172
00:13:56,321 --> 00:13:59,020
Hens with white feathers lay white eggs.

173
00:13:59,021 --> 00:14:02,220
Those with red feathers lay brown eggs.

174
00:14:02,221 --> 00:14:07,220
Breakfast Tech will return on Modern Marvels.

175
00:14:07,221 --> 00:14:16,400
We now return to Breakfast Tech on Modern Marvels.

176
00:14:16,401 --> 00:14:17,679
Aboard the USS John C.

177
00:14:17,680 --> 00:14:21,400
Stennis, it may take six hours for food service personnel to get

178
00:14:21,401 --> 00:14:25,510
to make breakfast, but it only takes a couple to devour it.

179
00:14:25,511 --> 00:14:27,600
Hundreds start their meal with

180
00:14:27,601 --> 00:14:32,520
orange juice, and by breakfast's end, sailors will have guzzled their way through more than

181
00:14:32,521 --> 00:14:34,640
75 reconstituted gallons.

182
00:14:34,641 --> 00:14:40,920
We're changing up the concentrated orange juice, which is at a 4 to 1 juice ratio with

183
00:14:40,921 --> 00:14:45,200
water that's read by a computer machine that is sent through a compressor and out to the

184
00:14:45,201 --> 00:14:46,640
soda machines out there.

185
00:14:46,641 --> 00:14:50,720
We use the distilling plant units down in the plants to purify ocean

186
00:14:50,721 --> 00:14:55,480
water, the salt water, so that way people can drink the water, and that way we are able

187
00:14:55,481 --> 00:14:58,940
to carry more juice on board and store for a longer time.

188
00:14:58,941 --> 00:15:04,920
The orange, one of nature's most simple and nourishing pleasures.

189
00:15:04,921 --> 00:15:07,440
To the Greeks, they were golden apples.

190
00:15:07,441 --> 00:15:10,220
To Romans, the food of the gods.

191
00:15:10,221 --> 00:15:16,580
For early Americans, oranges were a rare and expensive treat, reserved for special occasions.

192
00:15:16,581 --> 00:15:20,340
It was a big deal if you got in your Christmas stocking one orange.

193
00:15:20,740 --> 00:15:24,060
It was a big treat to try an orange, to taste an orange.

194
00:15:24,520 --> 00:15:28,060
In fact, what they would do to extend the orange juice

195
00:15:28,061 --> 00:15:31,420
was squeeze the orange juice and then add water and sugar.

196
00:15:31,600 --> 00:15:32,620
They made orange aid.

197
00:15:34,260 --> 00:15:36,100
Today, oranges are ubiquitous.

198
00:15:37,040 --> 00:15:38,360
Groomed from China to Brazil,

199
00:15:38,940 --> 00:15:41,780
the world now feasts on the fruit once reserved for royalty.

200
00:15:41,781 --> 00:15:48,780
In the U.S., Florida and California growers harvest nearly 25 billion oranges every year.

201
00:15:48,781 --> 00:15:52,780
In Brazil, the harvest is nearly twice that.

202
00:15:52,781 --> 00:15:58,780
And for good reason, for beneath its skin lies a power-packed assortment of vitamins and minerals,

203
00:15:58,781 --> 00:16:01,780
and as much potassium as a banana.

204
00:16:01,781 --> 00:16:07,780
Maybe that's why orange juice is on more American breakfast tables than any other juice.

205
00:16:07,781 --> 00:16:16,160
And it's the business of the Sunkist Juice Plant in Tipton, California, to make sure none of nature's nutrients are lost along the way.

206
00:16:16,620 --> 00:16:20,260
We handled the orange variety at this plant.

207
00:16:20,740 --> 00:16:25,880
The plant's capacity is about 1,800 tons a day, or about three truckloads an hour.

208
00:16:25,881 --> 00:16:33,440
We, in turn, offload that fruit, tilt the truck up to about a 25-degree angle, and let gravity carry the fruit out of the truck.

209
00:16:33,441 --> 00:16:40,800
As the oranges begin their shuffle toward the plant, random samples are captured through

210
00:16:40,801 --> 00:16:46,760
a trap door and sent to an on-site lab for testing.

211
00:16:46,761 --> 00:16:51,820
Oranges containing 11.5% sugar or higher are usually separated for concentrating.

212
00:16:51,821 --> 00:16:56,360
But the final factor is always the tester's taste buds.

213
00:16:56,361 --> 00:16:59,400
Only the best tasting oranges become single strength.

214
00:16:59,401 --> 00:17:02,400
All others become concentrate.

215
00:17:02,401 --> 00:17:08,300
Single strength juice is ready to drink, meaning that the customer is going to get freshly extracted

216
00:17:08,301 --> 00:17:14,160
juice and it does not go through any concentration processes.

217
00:17:14,161 --> 00:17:20,020
Separated for either single strength or concentrate, the oranges are prepped to enter the facility.

218
00:17:20,021 --> 00:17:24,060
Right down here where you see the water spraying on the fruit, we're washing the fruit.

219
00:17:24,061 --> 00:17:29,560
It's important that we do so, so that we can remove the residual sugars and surface contamination

220
00:17:29,561 --> 00:17:33,100
that gets on the orange from the time that it leaves the packing house until the time

221
00:17:33,101 --> 00:17:36,000
it arrives here.

222
00:17:36,001 --> 00:17:40,880
After spending nearly a year soaking up the sunshine, the oranges move out of the light

223
00:17:40,881 --> 00:17:43,560
and into the juice plant.

224
00:17:43,561 --> 00:17:48,260
Inside, inspectors remove defective and decaying oranges.

225
00:17:48,261 --> 00:17:53,580
Those that pass continue on toward a bed covered with thousands of tiny pins.

226
00:17:53,581 --> 00:17:57,680
Rolling atop the pins, the orange peels are punctured, just enough to release their precious

227
00:18:26,460 --> 00:18:27,660
oil.

228
00:18:27,680 --> 00:18:30,820
insect repellent on your patio, that sort of thing.

229
00:18:32,500 --> 00:18:35,660
The oranges' advance continues toward the extractors.

230
00:18:36,460 --> 00:18:40,240
They slide over a series of openings that swallow the fruit based on size.

231
00:18:40,880 --> 00:18:44,100
Smaller ones drop first, the larger ones seconds later.

232
00:18:45,720 --> 00:18:47,480
Into the extractors they fall.

233
00:18:49,600 --> 00:18:52,260
Designed after the same utensil is used in the home,

234
00:18:52,980 --> 00:18:55,740
oranges are cradled into a cup and sliced in half.

235
00:18:55,741 --> 00:19:00,740
The orange halves then move into a twisting cone-studded reamer.

236
00:19:00,741 --> 00:19:06,740
At a rate of 700 per minute, oranges are squeezed and the juice is released into a catch basin below.

237
00:19:06,741 --> 00:19:11,740
The peels fall onto a conveyor and are transported through a series of grinders,

238
00:19:11,741 --> 00:19:18,740
where they're minced into a fine powder, loaded on trucks, and later made into cattle feed.

239
00:19:18,741 --> 00:19:24,740
The orange peel is high in acetic acid. Acetic acid is a component of butter fat.

240
00:19:24,741 --> 00:19:31,740
So if you feed the cow orange peel, the cow is going to have a higher butterfat content in the milk,

241
00:19:31,741 --> 00:19:35,740
making the milk worth more to the dairyman.

242
00:19:35,741 --> 00:19:40,740
Meanwhile, the freshly extracted juice has hit a fork in the road.

243
00:19:40,741 --> 00:19:44,740
The single strength heads to cold storage for packaging.

244
00:19:44,741 --> 00:19:47,740
For concentrate, it's off to the evaporators.

245
00:19:47,741 --> 00:19:50,740
During the evaporating process, what we're doing under heat and vacuum

246
00:19:50,741 --> 00:19:55,780
vacuum is removing about 80 percent of the water that's in the juice so if you're starting with a

247
00:19:55,781 --> 00:20:01,220
gallon you're going to end up with about 20 percent of a gallon of juice to bring it back to single

248
00:20:01,221 --> 00:20:07,540
strength you merely have to add back that 80 percent to bring that back up to single strength

249
00:20:07,541 --> 00:20:14,980
orange juice since its development in the 1940s concentrating was the method that allowed most

250
00:20:14,981 --> 00:20:20,500
people to enjoy orange juice. That was until the 1950s when the founder of

251
00:20:20,501 --> 00:20:25,600
another juice giant, Tropicana, devised a way to keep orange juice fresher longer.

252
00:20:25,601 --> 00:20:30,340
Anthony Rossi was a Sicilian immigrant to the United States and he started out

253
00:20:30,341 --> 00:20:34,780
in the fruit distribution business. He started out shipping those wonderful

254
00:20:34,781 --> 00:20:39,160
crates of oranges and grapefruits up north from Florida and then he started

255
00:20:39,161 --> 00:20:43,700
to develop a way to squeeze the juice and send it up north and distribute it

256
00:20:43,701 --> 00:20:49,880
in glass containers. Rossi's team of scientists developed the process called

257
00:20:49,881 --> 00:20:55,200
flash pasteurization. The juice was quickly heated for 15 seconds, then

258
00:20:55,201 --> 00:21:01,540
chilled, which inactivated the natural enzymes, slowing the deterioration process.

259
00:21:01,541 --> 00:21:05,260
This allowed Tropicana to store their juice in glass bottles without

260
00:21:05,261 --> 00:21:09,980
refrigeration. Rossi also figured out a way to get his juice to the consumer

261
00:21:09,981 --> 00:21:11,840
without losing precious time.

262
00:21:12,640 --> 00:21:15,120
His specially insulated great white juice train

263
00:21:15,121 --> 00:21:17,320
still chugs across the East and Midwest.

264
00:21:19,180 --> 00:21:22,580
Today, the sterilization process enables freshly squeezed juice

265
00:21:22,581 --> 00:21:23,800
to survive even longer.

266
00:21:25,180 --> 00:21:28,200
Using an ingenious system called the shelf and tube method,

267
00:21:28,880 --> 00:21:31,960
the juice enters into a series of pipes at 35 degrees.

268
00:21:35,360 --> 00:21:39,880
Inside the sterilizer, the cold raw juice moves inside a clump of tubes

269
00:21:39,881 --> 00:21:42,440
positioned inside a single larger tube.

270
00:21:43,200 --> 00:21:46,120
The larger tube contains sterilized juice,

271
00:21:46,280 --> 00:21:48,980
which has been heated to 210 degrees Fahrenheit.

272
00:21:49,760 --> 00:21:52,880
As they pass each other, the juices share their temperatures,

273
00:21:53,620 --> 00:21:57,460
boosting the colder juice toward the 210 degrees needed for sterilization.

274
00:21:58,260 --> 00:22:03,040
After the juice has been sterilized and cooled back down to 35 degrees,

275
00:22:03,720 --> 00:22:07,500
it leaves the sterilizer and gets pumped directly

276
00:22:07,501 --> 00:22:09,780
into one of our 1 million gallon storage tanks.

277
00:22:09,880 --> 00:22:18,460
Sterilizing allows us to store the juice aseptically and for a long period of time.

278
00:22:18,840 --> 00:22:22,640
We can store juice in these tanks for up to a year without any flavor degradation.

279
00:22:23,140 --> 00:22:26,800
That gives us a chance to process the juice when we have it

280
00:22:26,801 --> 00:22:30,220
and then market the juice to the customers through the whole year.

281
00:22:31,500 --> 00:22:35,180
It will be pumped from the vats into tanker trucks and sent to market

282
00:22:35,181 --> 00:22:38,300
with nearly 95% of the orange's nutrients.

283
00:22:39,880 --> 00:22:46,340
Whether it's concentrated, fresh, or sterilized, every glass of orange juice still contains

284
00:22:46,341 --> 00:22:49,060
the nutritional value from the grow.

285
00:22:49,061 --> 00:22:56,320
90 calories, several essential vitamins, folate for the heart, and as much potassium as a

286
00:22:56,321 --> 00:22:59,820
banana.

287
00:22:59,821 --> 00:23:04,480
Another one of nature's gifts has also had a healthy effect on breakfast, despite having

288
00:23:04,481 --> 00:23:07,560
temporarily lost its way.

289
00:23:07,561 --> 00:23:14,560
In 1513, Spanish explorer Ponce de Leon brought the first orange seeds to Florida.

290
00:23:14,561 --> 00:23:20,560
Naval oranges were introduced to California in 1873 via a seedling from a Brazilian monastery.

